            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "## Details\n\nJSON Web Tokens (JWT) include the encryption algorithm used as part of the token payload. Further to this, some JWT implementations accept \"none\" as an encryption algorithm, meaning that the token is not encrypted. An attacker could use this to send a forged JWT token to the server and have it be accepted, if the application supports the usage of the \"none\" algorithm.\n\n### Best practices for prevention\n\n- Always enforce what algorithms your JWT verification function accepts\n- Do not support the JWT 'none' algorithm for signing or verification of tokens\n\n## Best practices for prevention\n* [Vulnerabilities in JWT](https://auth0.com/blog/critical-vulnerabilities-in-json-web-token-libraries/)",
                "text": ""
              },
